        "content": "# Audio Descriptions\n\n14 / 29\n\n.\n\nGiven anonymously. © 2026 Salvador\n\nDalí, Gala-Salvador Dalí Foundation /\n\nArtists Rights Society (ARS), New York \n\nNarrator: The artist Salvador Dalí made _The Persistence of Memory_ in 1931, using oil on canvas. The work measures 9 and a half inches high and 13 inches wide. In metric units, it is 24 centimeters high and 33 centimeters wide. It is set within a muted olive-colored mat and encased by a thin, dark brown wood frame. [...] Salvador Dalí was often associated with Surrealism, an artistic and literary movement that called on artists to explore the depths of the subconscious mind—a part of our mind that runs constantly without any filter or control. By fusing elements from his surroundings with the illogical details we might find in a dream, Dalí sought to, as he said, “discredit completely the world of reality.”\n\n   View this object in the collection\n\nTop\n\n[](",
